From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 04:30:09 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] split renderer: frame skipping, so the game and its audio
 keep their pace when drawing falls behind

The GL thread measures how far behind the walker it is (a moving average of the delay between a job's end and the GL thread
reaching it). Past 14 ms the guest is let run ahead instead of waiting for the drawing; past 28 ms a frame is left undrawn (at most
two in a row, for at most 120 ms), so the game's own clock and the audio it mixes stay on time. Back under 5 ms it waits for every
frame again. A skipped frame sends no list, draw, read-back or present to the renderer; the picture holds for a frame.

New setting perf.frame_skip (Auto / Off, Performance page, no restart). <PREFIX>_FRAME_SKIP=off|auto|always and the
FRAME_SKIP_*_US tuning variables are for tests. Early sync turns it off (no markers to measure by).

Known cost while running ahead: a texture the guest rewrites may show a frame early, and block-transfer read-backs of a skipped
frame are dropped.

Verified: portablekit_split_renderer_tests (a fake renderer with a chosen GL cost; four scenarios) and the settings tests pass; on
the desktop with an artificially slow GL thread (45 ms) the game holds 30 fps instead of 14 with about 40% of frames left undrawn
and no visible corruption; the recorded-mission replay gives identical results with frame skipping on and off (in both it no
longer reaches the expected outcome recorded on 10 Oct, which predates this change and is not explained yet).
Not verified: on the PS Vita.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

